## What was built

### Layout

```
streamlit_app.py                  entry point / Home page
pages/
  1_Score_Applicant.py            pick a test-set row or fill a form; set threshold
  2_Why_This_Decision.py          SHAP + LIME charts, reason codes
  3_What_If.py                    sliders for top-8 features, live re-scoring
  4_Global_Insights.py            model performance + Phase 2 SHAP figures
app_lib/
  common.py                       cached loaders, session-state, form-building
.streamlit/config.toml            theme (primaryColor, light base, minimal toolbar)
```

Streamlit's file-based multipage routing (`pages/` sibling to the entry script)
is used rather than the newer `st.navigation`/`st.Page` API, for broader
compatibility with the pinned Streamlit 1.39.

**Import path:** each entry file inserts the project root onto `sys.path` at the
top, before importing anything from `src` or `app_lib` — `.parent` in
`streamlit_app.py` (root/streamlit_app.py), `.parents[1]` in `pages/*.py`
(root/pages/X.py, one directory deeper). This avoids depending on Streamlit's
CWD/sys.path behaviour, which differs across versions.

### `app_lib/common.py` — shared app helpers
- `get_model_bundle()`, `get_full_data()`, `get_test_split()`, `get_codebook_cached()`
  — thin `st.cache_resource` / `st.cache_data` wrappers around `src.data` /
  `src.explain` (which already `lru_cache` the truly expensive objects — model,
  SHAP/LIME explainers — at the process level; these wrappers just save repeated
  DataFrame decoding on every Streamlit rerun).
- `score_applicant(applicant, threshold)` — one predict_proba call + decision.
- `default_applicant()` — dataset median (numeric) / mode (categorical) applicant.
- `ensure_state()` — guarantees `st.session_state.threshold` and `.applicant`
  exist no matter which page the user lands on first (Streamlit lets users jump
  straight to any page via the sidebar or a URL).
- `render_applicant_form(container, current, key_prefix, features)` — one
  `st.selectbox` (categoricals, options from the data) or `st.slider` (numerics,
  range = dataset min/max) per feature, with a stable `f"{key_prefix}_{feature}"`
  widget key.
- `reseed_form()` / `sync_whatif_defaults()` — solve the standard Streamlit
  gotcha where a keyed widget ignores a new `value=` after the key already
  exists in `session_state`. `sync_whatif_defaults` compares a signature of the
  current base applicant against the last-seen one and clears the What-if
  page's widget keys when it changes, so switching applicants on page 1 doesn't
  leave page 3's sliders showing stale data.

### Page 1 — Score an applicant
Threshold slider up top (drives every other page). Two modes: **pick from test
set** (row index → shows the true historical label, explicitly marked as not
seen by the model) or **enter manually** (20-field form seeded from the current
applicant, with "reset to dataset default" and "load current applicant" buttons).
Always ends with a P(default) / threshold / decision summary.

### Page 2 — Why this decision
Calls `explain_local(applicant, run_lime=True)` directly, renders
`local_shap_figure()` and `local_lime_figure()` (the Phase 2 plotting functions,
refactored to return `Figure` objects instead of only saving to disk — see
below) side by side via `st.pyplot`, then `reason_codes()` as a bullet list.
Closes each figure after rendering to avoid a matplotlib figure leak across
Streamlit reruns.

### Page 3 — What-if
Sliders/dropdowns for `global_importance().head(8)` — the 8 highest mean-|SHAP|
features — seeded from the current applicant, everything else held fixed.
Shows original vs modified P(default) with a `st.metric` delta, a "reset sliders"
button, an expander with the modified applicant's own SHAP chart (LIME skipped
here — it's ~1-3s per call, too slow for live slider dragging), and a "use this
as my current applicant" button that writes the scenario back into session state.

### Page 4 — Global insights
Pure `st.image()` of the Phase 1/2 pre-rendered PNGs plus the `metrics.json`
numbers — instant regardless of SHAP compute cost, since nothing here is
recomputed live.

### `src/explain.py` refactor
Split each local plotting function into a figure-builder (`local_shap_figure`,
`local_lime_figure`, returning `matplotlib.figure.Figure`) and a thin
save-to-disk wrapper (`_plot_local`, `_plot_lime`) that calls it and `savefig`s.
Phase 2's `python -m src.explain` behaviour is unchanged; Phase 3 reuses the
figure-builders directly instead of re-plotting from scratch.

